# Keep the Marine extension byte-identical on every platform.
#
# tauri.conf.json ships `../marine-extension` into the app verbatim, so whatever
# git writes into a checkout is exactly what reaches the user's browser. And
# three of those files are hashed: sw.js (`marineLoadGeneratedSkillAssets`)
# SHA-256s each asset under skills/scholay/generated/ and compares it against
# `assetHashes` in manifest.json. That check runs inside the PUT branch of
# `marineContextFetch`, *before* the fetch — so a mismatch does not corrupt a
# diff, it throws, the rime-context PUT is never sent, the comment target never
# publishes, and the 「生成」 button sits at 「准备中…」 until it fails with
# 「目标准备超时」. The message blames the input box and the local service; both
# are wrong, which makes this near-impossible to diagnose from the symptom.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true and CI checks out with a bare
# actions/checkout@v4 on windows-latest, so without this rule the assets arrive
# CRLF on the Windows runner and every hash misses — breaking comment generation
# on Windows builds only, while macOS builds work. Reproduced directly: a fresh
# clone under core.autocrlf=true yields CRLF and a mismatched hash without this
# file, and LF with a matching hash with it.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` is deliberately the ONLY rule. Marking the hashed assets
# `-text` looks stronger and is actually worse: it adds no checkout protection
# (this rule already pins LF regardless of core.autocrlf), but it disables
# check-IN normalization too. A contributor whose clone predates this file still
# has CRLF on disk — git does not rewrite existing files on pull — and under
# `-text` their next `git add` would commit those CRLF bytes into the index,
# breaking the hashes for everyone on every platform, permanently. Under
# `text=auto` that same `git add` normalizes back to LF and the situation heals
# itself.
donut/marine-extension/** text=auto eol=lf
